{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261025T182810Z-3266301c", "ts": "2026-10-25T18:34:10Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.7.3-dev.1", "branch": "feature/1052-evidence-transport"}, "body": {"base_commit": "3959446f0333b7b0f7f4a3ed5f71b9195a5ab8c1", "base_tree": "96319ae4ac7206764627ffe4a091408037aff1c4", "head_tree": "fd32e09ffca08ed065da822a6fbdf124fc9972fb", "head_commit": "cbab5222e088d08e458fbe1c1662e30dfa3fec59", "dispatch_commit": "cbab5222e088d08e458fbe1c1662e30dfa3fec59", "mode": "final (full review, ready for push)", "mode_chosen_by": "fallback-scope-widened", "stage": "inner", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/MIGRATION.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "tests/test_binary_skew_guard.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_ephemeral_worktree.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync_dispatch.py", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py", "tests/test_hot_path_git_batching.py", "tests/test_plan_check_claims.py", "tests/test_plan_hook_cmd.py", "tests/test_plan_import_status.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_pr_reviewer.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_session_start_ingest.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-status.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", "documentation/MIGRATION.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "tests/test_binary_skew_guard.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_ephemeral_worktree.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py", "tests/test_evidence_sync_dispatch.py", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py", "tests/test_hot_path_git_batching.py", "tests/test_plan_check_claims.py", "tests/test_plan_hook_cmd.py", "tests/test_plan_import_status.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_pr_reviewer.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_session_start_ingest.py", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-status.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "evidence configure --apply writes an exact (non-glob) notes fetch refspec, which very likely makes every plain `git fetch` fail on a remote with no notes ref yet; that case has no test", "recommendation": "Changed behaviour with no test, and probably a defect. `configure` adds `+refs/notes/prawduct:refs/notes/<remote>/prawduct` to `remote.<remote>.fetch`. In git, `get_ref_map` resolves each configured non-pattern refspec through `get_fetch_map(..., missing_ok=0)`, which dies with `fatal: couldn't find remote ref refs/notes/prawduct` when the source is absent. This is the same well-known failure a single-branch clone hits after its branch is deleted on the remote. `evidence_sync.sync` already special-cases this exact message for its explicit-refspec fetch (`_NO_REMOTE_REF`, 'the normal state before a first push'). Nothing covers the same lookup on the configured path. The onboard skill now runs `evidence configure --apply` right after onboarding whenever a remote exists, which is exactly when the remote has no notes ref. Doctor #25 offers it on any clone. So the likely effect is that `git fetch` and `git pull` (and git-tidy's fetch) fail in that clone until some clone pushes `refs/notes/prawduct`. The only plain-fetch test, `test_after_apply_a_plain_fetch_carries_the_notes`, pushes notes first, so it cannot see this. Write the red test first: `configure --apply` on a clone whose bare origin has no `refs/notes/prawduct`, then a plain `git fetch` must exit 0. If it fails, change the shape so a missing source is tolerated: a pattern refspec, which git skips when nothing matches, e.g. `+refs/notes/prawduct*:refs/notes/<remote>/prawduct*`; check `_notes_refs`' 4-part filter still holds. Or write the refspec only after the remote has the ref, e.g. from `sync` after a successful push. Scope: instance (the one writer of the refspec). Re-read onboard's and doctor #25's prose for the shape that ships.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "tests/test_evidence_sync.py"], "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "sync/configure report `ok` (exit 0) when git cannot even list the clone's remotes", "recommendation": "`evidence_sync._remote` returns `(None, reason)` for a failed `git remote` (rc != 0) as well as for 'no remote', and both `sync` and `configure` map that to `status: ok`. So a git failure exits 0. The PR skill relays only a non-zero exit, so this one is relayed as a sync that ran with nothing to do. The plan reserves `ok` for 'no remote'; a failing git is supposed to be reported under its step. Split the two: return an error (step `fetch` for sync) on rc != 0. That `err` is also echoed unscrubbed, unlike every other git detail in the module, which goes through `_git`'s `scrub_secrets`. Instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "`evidence status` counts facts held only on a fetched tracking ref as having no note", "recommendation": "`ingest` reads the local ref plus every `refs/notes/<remote>/prawduct`. But `_print_note_transport` decides presence from the local ref alone: `ref_present` checks only `refs/notes/prawduct`, and `unattached` \u2192 `_read_notes(project_dir, commits)` defaults to `(NOTES_REF,)`. Take a clone set up with `configure` plus a plain `git fetch`, or one that ingested at session start without a sync. It reports other clones' facts as 'with no note on their commit' or 'none \u2026 is on a note', though they arrived on a note. The suggested remedy (`evidence sync`) converges, so the cost is an alarming count, not lost data. Either read the tracking refs in `unattached` for the status count, or word the line as 'not on this clone's local notes ref'. Instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "evidence.py's own usage line still offers only status|list|ingest", "recommendation": "`evidence._CLI_USAGE` is what `prawduct-hook evidence <typo>` prints, and it omits `sync` and `configure`. The hook's `_USAGE` was updated and the grant sweep pins only that one, so nothing checks this copy. It is prose that nothing else reads. Delete the second roster (have the hook pass its own usage) or add the two ops; no other edit is wanted. Instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh runs `rm -rf $S/rt` with no argument check", "recommendation": "With the third argument omitted, `S` is empty. Under `set -e` the script then runs `rm -rf /rt` and `cd /rt` before anything fails. zsh does not word-split, so the reach is limited to `/rt`, but a verification script that deletes before it validates is a trap. Add `[[ $# -eq 3 ]] || { echo usage; exit 2; }` and quote the expansions. It is a dev tool that nothing executes in the suite. Instance.", "files": ["tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A remote named `prawduct` collides with the local notes ref", "recommendation": "`tracking_ref('prawduct')` is `refs/notes/prawduct/prawduct`, which git cannot hold beside `refs/notes/prawduct` (a directory/file conflict). Sync would fail at `fetch` with git's message. This is improbable. If it is worth anything, have `_remote` refuse that name with a reason. Otherwise accept it. Instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Two `evidence` routers with two usage lines; the lib's usage still offers only status|list|ingest", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the `evidence` command is now dispatched in two places. `cmd_evidence` in the hook routes `sync`/`configure`, and `evidence.evidence_cmd` routes the rest. Each has its own usage text. cbab5222 added sync and configure to the hook's `_USAGE`, but `evidence._CLI_USAGE` was not updated. That is the text a person sees for an unknown op (`prawduct-hook evidence bogus`) and for an `ingest` usage error, and it still reads `{status|list ...|ingest ...}`. The grant sweep's `_ops_offered` parses only the hook's `_USAGE`, so nothing catches this. Fix it through one owner rather than by editing the string: one router for all five ops with one usage line, or have `evidence_cmd` dispatch sync/configure lazily and drop the hook's special case. Per core.md: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "ingest and the unattached/status readers disagree on which refs are 'the notes'", "recommendation": "Scope: class. `ingest` reads the local ref plus every tracking ref (`_notes_refs`). `unattached` reads only `refs/notes/prawduct` (`_read_notes`' default), and so does `ref_present`, which `evidence status` uses. In the flow the docs now recommend (`evidence configure --apply`, a plain `git fetch`, then session-start ingest) the store fills from the tracking ref while the local ref is absent. `status` then prints 'no refs/notes/prawduct in this clone \u2014 none of the store's N transported fact(s) is on a note' and suggests `evidence sync` 'sends them', yet those facts arrived on a note. When a local ref exists, fetched facts not yet merged are counted as missing. The tracking-ref shape also has two hand-kept inverses: `tracking_ref()` builds it, and `_notes_refs` recognises only exactly-4-segment names, so a remote with a slash in its name is fetched and merged by sync but never read by ingest. Put the roster of refs in one owner and have both readers use it, or restrict 'missing' to facts this clone wrote.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The 'unsent-facts' view models the attach leg only; attached-but-unpushed is unobservable", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. A transported fact passes through three states: stored, attached to a local note, and pushed to the remote. `evidence status` reports only the second gap (no note), even though the plan calls it the unsent-facts count. The failure `/prawduct:pr` explicitly tolerates ('a non-zero exit is reported \u2026 and never blocks') is the push leg. After it, status reports 0 missing while nothing has left the clone, and the only signal is a line in one PR run's output. Comparing `refs/notes/prawduct` with `refs/notes/<push-remote>/prawduct` costs one local rev-parse and would make the third state observable. Either add that comparison or rename the count so it does not claim to measure 'sent'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`_EVIDENCE_WRITE_OPS` / `_DATA_PLANE_WRITE_OPS` is a third op roster that only a test reads", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The guards classify by the read set (a write is anything not in `_EVIDENCE_READ_OPS`), which is the right fail-closed design. The hook also ships a write roster that no production code reads, and the test pins that it equals the usage line minus the read set. The test already parses the usage line, so it can derive writes as offered minus reads and sweep those. That would delete two production constants and the agreement check that exists only to keep them in step.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_skill_command_grants.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Doctor #25 re-derives `evidence configure`'s logic in prose instead of running its preview", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Check #25 has the agent pick the remote by restating `gitstate.push_remote`'s rule ('the only remote, else origin, else the first listed'). It then reads both config keys itself and matches them against a refspec it spells out, and only after that runs `evidence configure`. The preview with no `--apply` already answers every branch: no remote gives ok plus a reason, configured gives 'nothing to change', otherwise it lists the missing keys. The prose copy is a second derivation that drifts when `push_remote` changes (its docstring already promises 'the remote a bare git push would reach', which `remote.pushDefault` would make false). It also costs three grants and is pinned by `test_evidence_transport_skills.py`. Make the step: run the preview, relay it, and on the owner's yes apply. Onboard's own `git -C <target> remote` check is redundant for the same reason.", "files": ["plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_evidence_transport_skills.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`evidence configure` without --apply is a pure read, but both guards refuse it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The preview writes nothing, but because `configure` is outside `_EVIDENCE_READ_OPS` the ephemeral guard and the skew guard both refuse it. The hook already has the opposite pattern for preview-by-default commands (`_EPHEMERAL_APPLY_GATED_COMMANDS`, `plan import-status`). This is low consequence because doctor runs in the main checkout, but it is an inconsistency inside one dispatcher. Either classify `configure` as apply-gated or state why it is refused.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "PR Step 5's sync instruction goes into an already very long single paragraph", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Framework Check 9 (Cumulative Health), 'key instructions buried in paragraphs'. Step 5 is one paragraph of roughly 600 words covering push, sync, the pushed-ref check, title and description, Closes lines, create, and the pr_number edit. The new sync step and its 'never blocks' carve-out sit inside it, between the push and the fail-closed check. Splitting Step 5 into ordered sub-steps would let an agent scan for what it must not skip.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh is a zsh-only script with an unguarded `rm -rf $S/rt`", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Every sibling tool is `#!/usr/bin/env python3`. This one hardcodes `/bin/zsh`, takes three positional arguments without checking them (an empty `$3` makes it `rm -rf /rt`), and assumes an `origin/develop` exists. It is a dev-only script, so this is a note. Add an argument check (`${3:?usage}`) or port it to the tools' language.", "files": ["tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh"], "oid": "O-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (evidence-transport, Chunks 02-06)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. For the owner, the plan's mid-build decisions and what settled each. (1) Chunk 04: `review_anchor` is made one home, and the merged check uses two `for-each-ref` calls rather than one `--is-ancestor`. Three holes in the Goals' lifecycle rule were sent back to the delegate and settled by LC1 plus 'authority fails closed'. This was an alignment-pass miss: the rule in Goals was incomplete. (2) Chunk 01: `evidence ingest` is refused in scratch worktrees, which withdraws the plan's 'runs anywhere' (an alignment-pass miss, R-15). The bound includes the merge base. (3) Chunk 02: reconcile and the unsent count live in `evidence_notes`. Sync runs git with LC_ALL=C and GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0, the stuck-merge check is ours, and `configure` uses `config --add`. A `chunk-imported` fact is matched to a branch by `actor.branch`. (4) Chunk 05: six budget ceilings were raised and none lowered. Ingest now reads the fetched tracking ref (97cb5fb8). One acceptance clause was struck because it named a Critic check that does not exist (an alignment-pass miss). (5) Chunk 03: the session-start line wording changed. Doctor #25 is a recommendation, not a check. The TYPE_CHECKING guard is kept, with a corrected comment. check-claims reclassifies a gone `working-branches:` entry under an in-progress plan as `missing`. (6) Chunk 06: the live round trip was scripted as tools/evidence-roundtrip.sh. priors: R-12 (the chunk-imported inference) and R-1 (stale evidence) were accepted; they are acknowledged here and not re-raised.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "oid": "O-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Goals still hedged: two 'Inferred' constraints, a Medium level whose named raise has no record, two builder-drafted [DECISION]s awaiting veto", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Several Goals the build now stands on are still unconfirmed. (a) 'Facts carry tree ids, not trees' is still marked *Inferred, owner can veto*. Chunks 01 and 02 shipped on it, so a review of an uncommitted tree reads as a gap in every other clone. (b) Not building tag containment is *Inferred*. (c) Level is Medium for \u00a7 5. The named raise was 'the owner reading the Lifecycle rule in Chunk 04 before C is briefed'. Nothing records that the reading happened, and Chunk 04 is built. (d) The two architecture.md [DECISION] records of 2026-10-25 (the egress site; `refs/notes` plus `.git/config` as the store's class) are 'builder-drafted for the owner's veto'. Get the owner's confirm-or-correct on (a)-(d) in one batch before `/prawduct:pr create`, which is where R-12's veto window closes for all of them. Then record each answer where the inference is stated. R-12's chunk-imported inference is accepted and is not re-raised here.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md"], "oid": "O-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Automated push of review facts to the product remote with no visibility check; the envelope carries the reviewer machine's absolute worktree path", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Learnings Cross-Check, core rule 'Before the first write to a shared surface (push, issue, PR, comment, artifact) after reading private sources, check its visibility and grep the payload'. Before this change, review facts never left the machine. Now `/prawduct:pr create|update` pushes them on every run to `refs/notes/prawduct` on the product remote, where anyone who can fetch the repo can read them. Each line is the full store envelope. That includes finding text, which reviewers can derive from private context such as memory, other repos' learnings, or owner rulings. It also includes `actor.worktree`, an absolute local path such as `/Users/<name>/source/...`. The local-first [DECISION] justifies this with 'no product content leaves its repository and owner'. That covers ownership but not visibility: on a public repo, facts that used to be private become public. security-model.md says 'findings text and file paths included' but does not mention the actor path. Either state the visibility question in the decision record for the owner's veto, or decide in code before the PR ships it to consumers. Options are stripping `actor.worktree` from transported lines or having sync check repo visibility.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-16"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A failed notes push leaves no durable signal: `evidence status` then reports 0 unsent facts", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. hook-surface rule: 'A governance checkpoint verifying a required side-effect must sit OUTSIDE the control flow that produces it'. The durable line `_print_note_transport` prints counts facts that have no note on their commit. That catches an attach that failed. It does not catch a push that failed. After `evidence sync` fails at `step: push`, which the PR skill reports and then 'goes on', every fact is attached locally. `evidence status` prints '0 transported fact(s) with no note on their commit', and the remedy text says sync 'sends them', so the clone reads as healthy. Meanwhile every other clone sees the branch uncovered. The loss is visible only in the output of the act that caused it, which is the gap Chunk 02's reconciliation was built to close. There is a cheap offline check: whether `refs/notes/prawduct` is contained in `refs/notes/<remote>/prawduct` (`merge-base --is-ancestor`, no network). Adding it to the status line would make unpushed notes visible.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py"], "oid": "O-17"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The notes ref has no lifecycle: it grows on the remote forever, and notes on rewritten-away commits are never pruned", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Every reviewed commit gains a note, and `cat_sort_uniq` merges only add lines. Nothing ever runs `git notes prune`. Consumer flows squash or rebase branches and then delete them, so their commits become unreachable while their notes stay, and every sync re-pushes them. Every boundary session start lists the whole ref (`git notes list` in `_read_notes`, then filters by the branch bound), so that cost grows with the repo's whole review history, not with the branch. The plan's NFR disposition bounds what ingest parses ('bounded by the branch's commits a bounded ingest walks'). It says nothing about the size of the ref itself, which also lives on the consumer's remote. State a lifecycle in data-model.md: either a prune step (e.g. `git notes --ref prawduct prune` before sync merges), or an accepted unbounded ref with a size line in `evidence status` beside the store's existing size probe.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "oid": "O-18"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "`_unfinished_merge` reports 'cannot locate the merge state' as an unfinished merge and prescribes `merge --commit`/`--abort`", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. hook-surface rule: 'derive a two-state message from the state, never write it for the one in mind'. When `git rev-parse --git-path` fails, `_unfinished_merge` returns the string '(cannot locate the notes merge state: \u2026)'. `sync` treats any non-None value as a stuck merge, so the operator reads 'a notes merge is unfinished ((cannot locate \u2026)) \u2014 finish it with `git notes \u2026 merge --commit` \u2026'. That points them at a merge that may not exist. Return the two cases distinctly, so the read failure surfaces as a failed step with its own reason.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py"], "oid": "O-19"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "rule-unenforced: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees' \u2014 three stale descriptions survive this wave, R-15's class last round", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Records Pass (misleads into action) plus Learnings Cross-Check. The core rule 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees' has no deterministic owner: `retired-claim-survives` checks only phrases a fragment declares under `Retires:`, and this fragment declares none. Instances in this interval: (1) In api-contract.md, `plan check-claims` still defines `release-pending` as 'the plan's scope has a pending change record \u2026 read through `release_readiness.pending_scopes`' and `missing` as 'gone with no pending record'. The code now judges by `plan_lifecycle.observe`, so a gone `working-branches:` entry under an in-progress plan with a pending fragment exits 1 where the contract says 0. An agent reading the contract will misread a degraded doctor #24. (2) api-contract.md and the new cross-cutting-concerns.md row list sync's failing `step` as fetch, merge, ingest and push. The code also returns `reconcile`, so a `--json` consumer switching on `step` misses a value. (3) Both records quote the session-start line as 'N review facts ingested from refs/notes/prawduct'; the code and its test print 'N evidence fact(s) ingested \u2026'. Last round's R-15 (the plan's 'runs anywhere') was the same class. Fix these three, then close the class with a construction rather than a longer list. Declare the retired wording as `Retires:` phrases in the fragment so record-lint catches survivors. Add a test that reads api-contract's step list against `evidence_sync`'s step literals. Search api-contract, data-model and cross-cutting for other descriptions of check-claims, sync and ingest.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md"], "oid": "O-20"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "check-claims reclassification is recorded as a build decision but not against additive-first, and the shipped fragment omits it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Records Pass ('it ships'), release rule 'A change-log entry's BODY must cover every chunk it shipped'. Chunk 03 changed what `plan check-claims` returns for one input. Before: a gone `working-branches:` entry whose scope had a pending fragment was `release-pending`, exit 0. Now it is `missing`, exit 1, whenever the plan's lifecycle is not merged-unreleased. In a consumer repo, doctor #24 can then flip from healthy to degraded. The Decisions block records the change as accepted. The plan's `governed_by` disposition for api-contract's additive-first norm still says 'nothing repurposed', and the norm's text forbids repurposing 'exit-code meanings'. Either say in that disposition why this is a corrected classification rather than a repurposed exit code, or record it as a ruling under the norm. The fragment's narrative and consumer note never mention check-claims, so the change would reach consumers without a release-note entry. Add one consumer-note line.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "oid": "O-21"}, {"g